text:
maria: Quick question — I'm adding KMS access for an EKS IRSA serviceaccount (payments svc) via Terraform. Do we prefer to add an aws_kms_grant or modify the key policy? Also, some of our secrets use an encryption context; do we need to enforce conditions on the grant? pinging @kevin @jen for guidance

kevin: Short answer: prefer key policy for long-lived cross-account or platform-level access. Use aws_kms_grant for ephemeral grants or when you can't modify the key policy (third-party keys, etc). But either way watch out for encryption context condition: if the decrypt call includes an EncryptionContext and the key policy/grant requires it, calls without it will fail.

jen: A few practical notes from previous outage — we had decrypt failures when secrets were created with context={"service":"payments"} but the consumer didn't supply it. If you plan to require context, make sure the app SDK sets it: e.g., JavaScript KMS decrypt call with {EncryptionContext: {service: 'payments', env: 'prod'}}. Otherwise don't require it.

maria: thanks. current plan: module creates kms key in platform account, alias redwood/payments, and we have a cross-account role in customer account (dedicated). The pods assume role via IRSA. How should trust and conditions look?

kevin: For IRSA trust we do federated principal with OIDC provider and condition on `StringEquals` for `sts:sub` or the `sub` claim mapping. Example trust JSON we use (terraform aws_iam_role with assume_role_policy):

```
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "Federated": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:oidc-provider/oidc.eks.example.com"
      },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "oidc.eks.example.com:sub": "system:serviceaccount:payments:payments-sa"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

kevin: For the KMS key, either modify key policy to allow that role's ARN to use kms:Decrypt/Encrypt/GenerateDataKey, or create a grant after the role exists. If you use key policy, also consider adding a condition limiting by `kms:EncryptionContext:service` if you want tighter scoping.

olivia: FYI encryption-context is a double-edged sword — great for scoping but brittle across client libs. We avoided requiring it for any cross-account secrets unless the client teams explicitly add ctx.

maria: terraform snippet I'm trying (aws_kms_grant approach). Can someone sanity check?

```
resource "aws_kms_grant" "payments_irsa" {
  name        = "payments-irsa-grant-${var.env}"
  key_id      = aws_kms_key.payments.id
  grantee_principal = aws_iam_role.payments_irsa_role.arn
  operations  = ["Decrypt","Encrypt","GenerateDataKey"]
  lifecycle {
    create_before_destroy = true
  }
}
```

jen: Looks fine. Two gotchas: 1) aws_kms_grant doesn't survive key rotation the same way key policy does; if you re-create the key you must recreate grants. 2) Some AWS SDKs (and Secrets Manager) may not honor waits for grant propagation — expect a ~5s-30s delay after creating the grant before Decrypt works. Add retry logic during rollout.

kevin: Also consider using `depends_on = [aws_iam_role.payments_irsa_role]` to avoid race with IRSA role creation. If you're doing cross-account, ensure the role ARN is exactly what you grant (no wildcards).

maria: Good tips. One more: do we need `aws:kms:ViaService` or any service principal for secrets manager access? The role will be used by pods directly talking to KMS via SDK, not via Secrets Manager.

jen: If Secrets Manager or SSM is in the path, you'd need to ensure the service principal (secretsmanager.amazonaws.com) is allowed if you're letting the service call KMS on behalf of the role. For direct SDK calls from pod role -> no extra service principal required, just allow the role.

